### Pre-flight Checklist

- [x] I have searched [existing issues](https://github.com/Gentleman-Programming/gentle-ai/issues) and this is not a duplicate
- [x] I understand that PRs will be rejected if the linked issue does not have `status:approved`

### 🔍 Affected Area

Documentation

### 💡 Problem Statement

Parent tracker #504 intends to install 11 native GitHub Copilot phase agents under `~/.copilot/agents/`, preferably as thin wrappers around the SDD skills already installed under `~/.copilot/skills/`.

The repository does not yet have organic runtime evidence that one wrapper contract works on both the current stable Windows runtime and a current stable POSIX runtime. Without that evidence, activating production assets would guess at file discovery, frontmatter selectors, skill resolution, delegation, and caller-model inheritance.

This issue must establish the wrapper contract before any production capability or asset activation.

### 📦 Proposed Solution

Create a focused, sanitized runtime-proof report for one candidate thin-wrapper format.

Test the exact same candidate wrapper bytes on:

1. Current stable Windows.
2. Exactly one current stable POSIX environment: Linux or macOS.

Record for each environment:

- test date;
- OS version and architecture;
- exact VS Code stable version;
- exact GitHub Copilot extension version;
- candidate `.agent.md` bytes;
- observed global discovery path;
- accepted frontmatter and tool-selector spelling;
- delegation transcript or equivalent sanitized evidence;
- caller-model inheritance evidence;
- every failure or limitation.

The proof must verify:

- `~/.copilot/agents/*.agent.md` is discovered organically by VS Code Copilot;
- no Copilot-specific coordinator agent is required;
- an ordinary Copilot session can delegate to a phase wrapper;
- all 11 canonical names are discoverable: `sdd-init`, `sdd-explore`, `sdd-research`, `sdd-propose`, `sdd-spec`, `sdd-design`, `sdd-tasks`, `sdd-apply`, `sdd-verify`, `sdd-archive`, and `sdd-onboard`;
- the thin wrapper can resolve and follow `~/.copilot/skills/<phase>/SKILL.md`;
- omitting `model` inherits the caller model, demonstrated with two caller-model selections when the runtime exposes that observation;
- one valid direct Copilot-native model selector routes successfully;
- no named profile, static model-name map, TUI flow, or custom coordinator is required.

Add the resulting report as repository documentation. Do not modify the production capability manifest, adapter activation, production asset embed, install/sync/uninstall behavior, model-assignment state, or TUI.

Acceptance is binary:

- PASS: both environments satisfy the same thin-wrapper contract.
- FAIL: record the exact mismatch and leave wrapper architecture unapproved.

The recorded versions are “tested with” versions only. Do not infer or document a minimum supported-version floor.

### 🔄 Alternatives Considered

- Activate the wrappers from documentation alone: rejected because the target behavior is runtime-owned and platform-sensitive.
- Test Windows only: rejected because the production path is cross-platform.
- Require multiple POSIX systems before choosing the wrapper shape: deferred; one POSIX system is sufficient for architecture selection, but not for a broad support-floor claim.
- Reuse the historical #505/#708 assets as proof: rejected because those branches are closed, predate the current 11-phase and capability-manifest contracts, and do not record exact current stable runtime versions.

### 📎 Additional Context

Parent tracker: #504.

This is a proof/documentation work unit. It must remain below the 400 changed-line review budget and must not activate user-visible production behavior.

### Pre-flight Checklist

- [x] I have searched [existing issues](https://github.com/Gentleman-Programming/gentle-ai/issues) and this is not a duplicate
- [x] I understand that PRs will be rejected if the linked issue does not have `status:approved`

### 🔍 Affected Area

TUI (terminal UI)

### 💡 Problem Statement

### The **Gentle AI logo** currently uses a hardcoded **`magenta`** color in the OpenCode TUI.

This can create visual inconsistency when users select an OpenCode theme with a different primary color, such as blue, green, or orange. The rest of the interface follows the selected theme, while the Gentle AI logo remains magenta.

It would be better for the logo to adapt to the active OpenCode theme.


### 📦 Proposed Solution

Replace the hardcoded `magenta` color with the active OpenCode theme's primary color.

Instead of:

```tsx
<text fg="magenta">{line}</text>
```

The logo component can receive the current theme color:

```tsx
<text fg={props.color}>{line}</text>
```

And the color can be provided from the OpenCode TUI API:

```tsx
<Logo color={api.theme.current.primary} />
```

This would make the Gentle AI logo automatically match the user's selected OpenCode theme while keeping the existing logo design unchanged.

Complete example:

```tsx
// @ts-nocheck
/** @jsxImportSource @opentui/solid */
import type { TuiPlugin } from "@opencode-ai/plugin/tui"
import { useTerminalDimensions } from "@opentui/solid"
import { createMemo } from "solid-js"

const id = "gentle-logo"

const roseArt = [
  "             ⣠⣾⣷⣶⣦⣤⣤⣄⣠⣄⣀  ⢀⣀⣀",
  "          ⢀⣴⣿⣿⠿⣋⣭⣭⣯⣭⣍⣭⣿⣟⠛⠛⠿⠿⣿⣷⣄",
  "      ⢀⣴⣾⡟⢻⣿⡟⠁⣼⣿⠏⣵⢻⣿⣻⣿⣿⢿⡻⣿⣿⣶⡌⢿⣿⣷⣦⣤⡄",
  "   ⣤⣶⣾⣿⣿⠏ ⠈⢿⣄ ⢹⣏⠠⠟⣾⣿⣿⣿⣿⣿⠷⣏⣼⠟⢡⣿⡟⠋⢻⣿⣿⡄",
  "   ⠈⣿⣿⣿⣿⡆   ⣽⢧⡘⠈⠳⣦⣍⠛⠛⢦⣉⣴⣛⣫⣭⣴⡟⠋  ⣾⣿⣿⡿",
  "   ⢀⠹⣿⣿⣿⣷⣤⡄ ⠋ ⠙⢆ ⣠⠴⠟⠛⣛⣛⣛⠟⠋⠁⠺⡇ ⣀⣴⣿⣿⡟⠁",
  "   ⠈⣀⠈⠛⠷⠿⣿⣿⣷⣤⣀ ⢠⠋   ⠈⠉⠉    ⣠⣴⣥⠾⠛⠉⣰⣿⣷",
  "          ⠹⣯⣝⠛⠛⠷⢶⣤⣤⣀   ⢀⡠⠖⠋⠉⢉⣀⣀⣴⣾⣿⠿⠟⠃ ⠠⠦",
  "⠁       ⠖  ⠘⠻⢿⣦⣄⡀  ⠉⠛⢦⠠⢊⠤⠴⢒⣛⣛⣩⣽⡿⠟⠁⢀⡀",
  "⠲⠶⣦⠴⠶⠶⠶⠶⡶⠶⢶⣤⣄⡀⠨⠭⠽⠟⣓⢦⣀⠈⢇⡥⠖⠛⠋⠉⠉⠉    ⠈  ⢠⡤",
  "  ⠈⢷ ⠐⠂⢤⣽⣄ ⠰⡎⠙⠳⣄⡀ ⠈⢣⠘⢦⠋⣀⡬⠟⠛⠛⠉⢀⣀⣀⣠⡤⠄⠃",
  "   ⠈⢳⣀⡒⠉⠉⣉⠙⡲⣽⣄ ⣏⠳⡄ ⠘⡇ ⡾⠁ ⢀⡤⠖⣻⣿⡏⢡⡎ ⠰⠄",
  "     ⠛⠻⢦⣄⣉⡁⣀⣀⣈⣙⣺⣌⡇⢠⢀⡇⡾  ⣴⣿⡷⠊ ⢲⣠⠟",
  "          ⠈⠉    ⠈⠳⡄⣸⢱⠇⢀⣰⣯⣭⣥⠭⠾⠛⠃",
  "                  ⡷⠡⡯⢖⠉   ⢠⠤",
  "                ⡠⢊⡴⠤⠂⠃ ⠒",
  "             ⢀⡴⢪⠔⣉⠔⠋",
  "               ⠐⠈",
]

const compactArt = ["✦ Gentle AI ✦"]

const Logo = (props: { color: any }) => {
  const dim = useTerminalDimensions();
  const lines = createMemo(() => {
    const term = dim();
    return term.height >= roseArt.length + 6 && term.width >= 64 ? roseArt : compactArt;
  });

  return (
    <box flexDirection="column" alignItems="center">
      {lines().map((line) => (
        <text fg={props.color}>{line}</text>
      ))}
    </box>
  );
};

const tui: TuiPlugin = async (api) => {
  api.slots.register({
    id,
    order: 100,
    slots: {
      home_logo() {
        return <Logo color={api.theme.current.primary} />;
      },
    },
  });
};

const plugin = { id, tui };

export default plugin;
```

### 🔄 Alternatives Considered

An alternative would be to keep the current hardcoded magenta color, but this can visually conflict with user-selected OpenCode themes. Using the active theme's primary color provides better visual consistency.

### 📎 Additional Context

### Example

If a user selects a blue OpenCode theme, the Gentle AI logo would automatically appear blue.

If they switch to a green theme, the logo would automatically appear green.

This keeps the Gentle AI logo visually consistent with whichever OpenCode theme the user chooses, instead of always displaying it in magenta.


