### Problem

On Windows, the `gentle-pi` postinstall always exits 1, which makes pnpm abort the whole install. Every `pi update --extensions` / `pi update --all` fails, and the package manifest is left out of sync with what is on disk.

The native binary installs fine. The step that fails is `installTuiModeSetting()` in `scripts/install-tui-mode-setting.mjs`, which reports `settings.json changed while opening`. There is no concurrent writer. The identity check itself is what cannot pass on Windows.

`readSettings()` opens the file and verifies it is still the same file:

```js
const before = inspect(path);            // lstatSync
const fd = openSync(path, constants.O_RDONLY | (constants.O_NOFOLLOW ?? 0));
if (!sameFile(before, fstatSync(fd))) throw new Error("settings.json changed while opening");
```

and `sameFile()` requires both `dev` and `ino` to match:

```js
function sameFile(left, right) {
	return left && right && left.dev === right.dev && left.ino === right.ino;
}
```

On Windows, Node's `lstat()` reports `dev = 0` while `fstat()` on a handle to that same file reports the real volume id. `ino` matches on both. So `left.dev === right.dev` compares `0` against a nonzero device id and is always `false`, and the function reports "different file" for a file compared against itself.

This is deterministic, not a race: I sampled the inode and mtime of `settings.json` once a second for six seconds during a failing install and neither changed. The failure reproduces on every install attempt and on any path, including a freshly created file in the temp directory that no other process knows about.

Consequences:

- `pi update --all` cannot complete on Windows while `gentle-pi` is installed.
- pnpm rolls back on the failed lifecycle script, so `package.json` and `pnpm-lock.yaml` keep the previous version while `node_modules` already holds the new one. My install ended up with `node_modules/gentle-pi` at 3.3.0 and both `package.json` and `pnpm-lock.yaml` still pinned to 3.1.1. Unrelated packages in the same install get rolled back too: `gentle-engram` went back from 0.1.14 to 0.1.13.
- `pi update --extension npm:gentle-pi@3.3.0` then reports `Updated npm:gentle-pi@3.3.0` while the pinned manifest keeps pnpm on the old version, so the failure is easy to misread as a successful update.
- There is no opt-out. `GENTLE_PI_SKIP_GENTLE_AI_INSTALL=1` skips only native provisioning; `install-gentle-ai.mjs` still runs `installTuiModeSetting()` afterwards, and it is the only remaining step that can set `process.exitCode`.

A two-line change to `sameFile()` fixes it without weakening the check: compare `ino` first, and treat a `dev` of `0` as "this platform does not report a device id from `lstat`" rather than as a mismatch.

```js
function sameFile(left, right) {
	if (!left || !right || left.ino !== right.ino) return false;
	if (left.dev === 0 || right.dev === 0) return true;
	return left.dev === right.dev;
}
```

I verified this keeps the identity check honest: `lstat`/`fstat` of the same file returns `true`, two `lstat` calls on the same file return `true`, a missing stat returns `false`, and a different file in the same directory still returns `false`. The relaxation only affects the `lstat`-vs-`fstat` comparison in `readSettings()`; the other two `sameFile()` call sites (`original.stat` vs `latest.stat`, and the lock directory check) compare two `lstat` results, where both sides are `0` on Windows and unchanged elsewhere.

I am running this as a local `pnpm patch` and can open a PR with the change plus a regression test if that is useful.

### Steps to reproduce

Standalone reproduction, no `gentle-pi` install required. It copies `sameFile()` and the `readSettings()` identity check verbatim and runs them against a file it creates itself, so nothing else can be touching it:

```js
