## Summary

`extensions/skill-registry.ts` scans each configured skill root (e.g. `.agents/skills`) for **immediate child directories only**, looking for a `SKILL.md` directly inside each one. It does not recurse further. As a result, skills organized into category subdirectories — e.g. `.agents/skills/infra/gcloud/SKILL.md`, `.agents/skills/delivery/asc-cli-usage/SKILL.md` — never show up in the generated `.atl/skill-registry.md`, even though Pi's own top-level `<available_skills>` injection does see them (that mechanism differs from the registry generator).

This matters specifically for the delegator workflow the registry exists to support: parents read `.atl/skill-registry.md` to pick exact `SKILL.md` paths to pass to subagents. Category-nested skills are structurally invisible to that workflow, so subagents never get them injected, while the parent session (via Pi core) sees them fine — a confusing split.

## Evidence

Relevant scan logic (approximate, from the installed `gentle-pi@2.1.2` package):

```ts
for (const entry of entries) {
  const skillDir = join(root, entry.name);
  // ...
  if (!dirInfo.isDirectory()) continue;
  const candidate = join(skillDir, "SKILL.md");
  // ...
  if (skillInfo.isFile()) out.push(candidate);
}
```

This only looks one level deep from each configured root (`skill-registry.ts:97-126` in the installed package). Category subdirectories containing multiple skill folders (rather than a `SKILL.md` directly) are silently skipped — no warning, no partial-index note.

## Suggested fix

Make the scan bounded-recursive: if an immediate child directory does not itself contain `SKILL.md`, look one (configurable, small) level deeper for `*/SKILL.md` before giving up on that branch. This preserves the current fast/flat case while supporting a common "categorize skills into subfolders" organization pattern without requiring projects to flatten their skill trees to work around the generator.

## Workaround currently in use

We flattened category subdirectories (`infra/`, `delivery/`) back to the skill root as an interim fix. This works but gives up the organizational grouping we'd like to keep, purely to satisfy the registry generator's scan depth.

## Environment

- `gentle-pi@2.1.2` (npm), installed via Pi package manager.
- Reproduced by placing `SKILL.md` files two levels deep under a configured skill root and running `/skill-registry:refresh` — those skills are absent from the regenerated `.atl/skill-registry.md`, while flat top-level skills at the same root appear correctly.
